## Zone System — Canonical Definitions

Zones are organized by **frequency of human use and energy flow**, not by distance alone. They represent the intensity of management required.

### Zone 0 — The Dwelling
- **Definition:** The house, living space, and its immediate envelope.
- **Energy center:** All design decisions radiate from here.
- **Contains:** Kitchen, pantry, passive solar features, indoor plants, greywater sources.
- **Management:** Daily; highest human energy input.
- **App label:** `Zona 0 – Casa`
- **POI types applicable:** House, Greenhouse (attached), Greywater system, Rainwater tank (roof-integrated), Solar panels, Compost toilet.

### Zone 1 — Intensive Kitchen Garden
- **Definition:** The most frequently visited area; receives daily attention.
- **Purpose:** Salad crops, herbs, seedling nursery, cold frames, beehives, small composters.
- **Design features:** Herb spirals, keyhole beds, mandala gardens, vertical growing structures.
- **Management:** Daily to every other day; weeding, harvesting, watering.
- **App label:** `Zona 1 – Jardim Intensivo`
- **POI types applicable:** Herb spiral, Keyhole bed, Cold frame, Greenhouse (detached), Beehive, Worm farm, Small compost, Nursery, Rainwater barrel.

### Zone 2 — Productive Orchard & Semi-Intensive Systems
- **Definition:** Area visited several times per week.
- **Purpose:** Orchards, food forest establishment, larger vegetable patches, small livestock (chickens, ducks, rabbits, pigs in rotation).
- **Design features:** Food forest layers (canopy → ground cover), chicken tractors, integrated pest management (IPM), nurse plants, pioneer species.
- **Management:** Several times per week.
- **App label:** `Zona 2 – Uso Frequente`
- **POI types applicable:** Orchard, Food forest patch, Chicken coop, Duck pond, Rabbit hutch, Pig arc, Compost system (large), Swale (minor), Annual bed (large), Cover crop area.

### Zone 3 — Main Crops & Extensive Cultivation
- **Definition:** Area visited once or twice per week.
- **Purpose:** Staple crops (grains, legumes, roots), larger livestock paddocks, silvopasture systems, bulk compost production, agroforestry corridors.
- **Design features:** Keyline water design, swales, windbreaks, contour planting, rotational grazing cells.
- **Management:** Weekly; lower labor intensity per hectare.
- **App label:** `Zona 3 – Uso Ocasional`
- **POI types applicable:** Crop field, Pasture, Swale (main), Contour berm, Windbreak, Silvopasture, Dam/Pond (farm-scale), Biogas digester, Barn, Grain storage.

### Zone 4 — Semi-Wild Managed Land
- **Definition:** Area visited occasionally; light human management only.
- **Purpose:** Managed woodland for fuel and timber, wild foraging, seed saving from escaped volunteers, apiculture (extended foraging range), water catchment headwaters.
- **Design features:** Coppice systems, pollarding, shelterbelts, controlled burns (fire management), wildlife habitat corridors.
- **Management:** Monthly to seasonally.
- **App label:** `Zona 4 – Uso Mínimo`
- **POI types applicable:** Coppice area, Timber stand, Wild foraging patch, Apiary, Water catchment, Retention basin, Wildlife corridor.

### Zone 5 — Wilderness Reserve
- **Definition:** Unmanaged; human role is **observation only**.
- **Purpose:** Biodiversity reservoir, climate buffer, spiritual retreat, scientific baseline, source of wild genetics.
- **Rules:** No cultivation, no livestock, no harvesting except for observation and seed collection.
- **Contains only:** Natural vegetation, wildlife, native seed bank, natural water flow.
- **App label:** `Zona 5 – Silvestre`
- **App constraint:** No `culture` or `animal` elements allowed in Zone 5 (enforce in UI and store).

